# Part B of guide rows.
def load(A):
    A("bath-remodel-questions","Questions before a bathroom remodel","Remodels","bathroom-plumbing",
      "Plumbing decisions to settle before a bathroom is opened up.",
      "Same place or a new place",
      "A new faucet and toilet in the same spots is a different project from moving a shower to another wall. Moving a toilet means moving the flange and the vent relationship. Moving a shower means a new trap and a valve at the right height. Bathroom installation and remodel work is on the BBB list.",
      "Ask who opens the wall and who closes it. Ask whether old shutoffs will be replaced while they are exposed. Measure the door swing before a larger toilet is chosen.",
      "Added baths in old cottages",
      "Many coast cottages have a bath that was once a porch. Drains may run flat and vents may be small. A slow drain should be discussed before tile goes up. Pipe inspection before demo belongs in the estimate if the drains have already been a problem.",
      "Ask how the permit and inspection are handled. Keep the written scope.",
      ["Decide which fixtures move.","Ask who patches and paints.","Replace tired shutoffs while the wall is open.","Ask about the permit."])
    A("kitchen-remodel","Kitchen remodel plumbing","Remodels","kitchen-plumbing",
      "Sink, dishwasher, and supply decisions to make before cabinets are ordered.",
      "Moving the sink",
      "An island sink needs a vent and a drain path that a wall sink already had. That is often the big plumbing change. A new faucet in the same place, with a dishwasher beside it, is simpler if the trap and supplies are sound. Kitchen installation and remodel work is listed.",
      "Dishwasher drains need a high loop or an air gap. A refrigerator line needs a stop you can reach after the fridge is in. A pot filler is a supply in a hot zone and needs its own stop. Name these at the estimate.",
      "While the wall is open",
      "Old valves snap when they are first moved. Plan on new angle stops. If the open wall shows galvanized pipe, decide then whether the job grows into a re-pipe. Opening the wall twice costs more than deciding once.",
      "Photos of an open wall can go to office@plumbing101.net if the project is already underway.",
      ["Say if the sink moves to an island.","Include the dishwasher, fridge line, and pot filler.","Plan on new angle stops.","Decide about old galvanized while the wall is open."])
    A("rough-in","New construction plumbing rough-in","Remodels","new-construction-plumbing",
      "What a rough-in is, and what has to be decided before it is covered.",
      "Open walls, then finish",
      "On a new building the drains, vents, and supplies go in while the framing is open. Inspection happens before cover. Finish fixtures wait for cabinets and paint. New plumbing construction is on the BBB profile. The sequence depends on a settled fixture layout.",
      "Changes after a slab or after walls close are the costly kind. Pick toilet locations, shower sizes, and sink walls on the plan. A small shop and a house do not share a fixture count, so bring the plan.",
      "Coordination",
      "The plumber, the framer, and the concrete work have to agree on penetrations. Ask whose name is on the permit. This website does not assign you a project manager. That is settled in the estimate.",
      "Call on a weekday with the plan set. A request that only says pipes is hard to price.",
      ["Bring a fixture plan.","Settle locations before the slab.","Ask whose name is on the permit.","Separate rough-in from finish."])
    A("small-shop-drains","Sinks in a small shop","Remodels","commercial-plumbing",
      "Notes for a small coastal shop or cafe, using the commercial services that are actually listed.",
      "Not a house sink",
      "A commercial sink may need a larger drain and, for food service, a grease interceptor sized by the local authority. A house trap under a mop sink will not stand up to a restaurant. The BBB profile includes commercial plumbing and drain service. It does not print an interceptor size. That comes from the use and the local rules.",
      "Floor drains in a slow season dry out and smell. Refill them with water. If they smell and they are slow, call. Do not dump a gallon of chemical opener into a drain that may reach a small interceptor.",
      "Leases and closets",
      "The lease often says who pays for plumbing inside the walls. Read it before a remodel. A heater sharing a closet with a mop sink is a tight space. Photos help.",
      "Call with the town and the type of business. New construction and remodel are both listed.",
      ["Name the use, especially food.","Ask the landlord who owns the repair.","Refill dry floor drains.","Photo the heater closet."])
    A("pipe-inspection","What a pipe inspection includes","Inspections","pipe-inspection",
      "What pipe inspection and evaluation means, since that phrase is on the BBB profile.",
      "Looking, not guessing",
      "Evaluation means looking at what is accessible and, when the scope says so, running a camera in a drain. It separates a fixture clog from a broken sewer, and it supports a re-pipe decision. It is not a speech in place of a look.",
      "Ask whether the visit includes supplies, a drain camera, a heater check, or all three. Ask how you will get the findings. Notes or marked photos last longer than a porch conversation.",
      "Prepare the house",
      "Clear the hatch, the heater closet, and the cleanout if you know it. Mention pets, locks, and low clearance. If a purchase deadline is involved, say so, and remember hours end at 3:30 p.m. on weekdays.",
      "Write CCB 232697 on your own notes and verify the license on the state site before a large job.",
      ["Ask if a camera is included.","Clear hatches and closets.","Ask for notes or photos.","Share a purchase deadline."])
    A("prepare-the-visit","Prepare for a plumber visit","Hiring","residential-plumbing",
      "A short setup so a weekday visit is spent on the repair.",
      "Clear access",
      "Move storage away from the sink, the toilet, the heater, and the hatch. Unlock gates. Crate pets. On a rental, have someone who can approve a repair or reach the owner quickly.",
      "Write when the problem started, which fixtures are involved, and what you already tried. If you used a chemical opener, say so. If the main is off, say that at the door.",
      "The clock",
      "Posted hours are 8:30 a.m. to 3:30 p.m., Monday through Friday. The phone is (541) 614-1344 and the email is office@plumbing101.net. Include the town in a written request.",
      "Payment terms belong on the estimate you accept. This site does not invent them.",
      ["Clear the work area.","Secure pets.","Disclose chemical openers.","Keep the owner reachable on a rental."])
    A("describe-a-leak","How to describe a leak","Hiring","residential-plumbing",
      "A few words that aim a plumbing visit correctly.",
      "Supply or drain",
      "Supply water runs whenever a valve is open to pressure. It is usually clear, and it continues while fixtures sit idle until you close a valve. Drain water shows up when something is used: a flush, a shower, a washer. It may be soapy or dirty. Saying which one you have is the most useful sentence.",
      "Then say where and how fast: a drip, a pencil stream, or a spray. Say whether a valve is already closed.",
      "Photos",
      "Three photos are enough to start: the whole area, the wet fitting, and the damage. Email them to office@plumbing101.net with your number and town. Do not stand in water to photograph a panel or a heater control.",
      "Name the town in the first sentence. The company serves both counties, and the drive is part of scheduling.",
      ["Say supply or drain.","Say the room and how fast it leaks.","Say which valve you closed.","Email a few photos with the town."])
    A("read-an-estimate","How to read a plumbing estimate","Hiring","pipe-inspection",
      "What a written estimate should make clear before you say yes.",
      "Scope in plain sentences",
      "Look for the fixtures and pipes included, the material if pipe is being replaced, and what is left out. Wall patch, paint, permits, and haul-away are the items people assume are included. Ask. A supply re-pipe is not a sewer replacement unless the paper says so.",
      "Put license 232697 on your copy and check the state record yourself. The payment schedule belongs on that paper. This website will not quote a price the company has not given you.",
      "Changes",
      "Ask who pulls the permit, how long the water will be off, and whether a toilet can stay in service. If an open wall shows more damage, the change in scope should be written before the extra work starts.",
      "Compare bids by scope, not only by the last number.",
      ["Match the paper to the fixtures you discussed.","Ask about patching and permits.","Check CCB 232697 yourself.","Get scope changes in writing."])
    A("permits","Asking about plumbing permits","Hiring","new-construction-plumbing",
      "Why you should ask who handles the permit, without guessing your town's fee.",
      "The job decides",
      "A faucet supply line and a whole re-pipe are not the same kind of work. Local building departments and the state building-codes framework set the rules. Ask the plumber, before work starts, whether your job needs a permit and whose name goes on the application.",
      "A permit is a record that the work could be inspected. It matters when you sell, and it matters if a hidden joint fails later. It is a fair question.",
      "What this page will not invent",
      "It will not list fees or counter hours. Those change. Call the building department for the official answer, and ask Plumbing 101, LLC how they handle paperwork on jobs they accept. New construction is where permits are routine.",
      "Keep the permit number with the estimate.",
      ["Ask if this job needs a permit.","Ask who applies.","Call the building department for the official rule.","File the number with the estimate."])
    A("read-the-ccb","How to read CCB license 232697","Hiring","residential-plumbing",
      "A plain reading of the public Oregon CCB summary for Plumbing 101, LLC.",
      "The lines on the summary",
      "The official summary lists PLUMBING 101 LLC, license 232697, status Active, first licensed October 19, 2020. Unpaid claims and unpaid civil penalties are shown at $0.00. The same summary says complaints have been filed in the past 10 years, and that there has been an administrative suspension for lack of proof of liability insurance. Disciplinary sanctions are marked no.",
      "The board explains that an administrative insurance suspension is about proof of financial security, and that a license is reinstated when proof is received. Read current status first, and read the history too. One public complaint record was a 2023 owner claim on a small commercial remodel, closed as settled by telephone mediation.",
      "Use the state page",
      "Directory sites sometimes add bond amounts or star counts this website does not copy, because those figures conflict or were not on the official summary. Use search.ccb.state.or.us for license 232697.",
      "The company phone is still (541) 614-1344. Hiring is your decision after you read the record.",
      ["Read status first.","Read the complaint and suspension lines too.","Prefer the official CCB page.","Put the license number on your contract."])
    A("who-is-listed","People named on the public profile","Hiring","residential-plumbing",
      "The names on the BBB profile, and the biographies this site does not invent.",
      "Two names",
      "The BBB profile lists Faith A. Worthington as owner/manager and Michael E. Worthington as owner/member. It lists an LLC incorporated August 21, 2020, with a business start date of October 19, 2020, the same day the CCB license was first issued.",
      "This site does not add personal biographies, extra staff names, or years of individual trade experience. A business-registry mirror spells an agent name differently and lists several past addresses. Those are not printed here as a shop address.",
      "What accreditation is",
      "BBB accreditation dates from October 31, 2025, with an A+ rating on that profile. The bureau describes accreditation as a standards commitment. It is not a promise about one future job. You should still get a written scope and still check license 232697.",
      "Call during posted hours and ask who will come to the house.",
      ["Read the BBB management lines yourself.","Do not expect staff biographies here.","Check the CCB license anyway.","Ask who will do the work."])
    A("install-day","A water heater replacement visit","Water heaters","water-heaters",
      "What to arrange before a water heater installation, without a made-up duration.",
      "Facts from the label",
      "The plumber needs the fuel, the size if it is on the label, the room, and whether a pan and a drain exist. A photo of the label and a photo of the piping above the heater answer most of that. Measure a closet doorway. Some tanks do not turn the corner.",
      "Water is off for part of the visit, and a new tank needs time to heat. Ask how long your house will be without hot water. This page will not print a stock number of hours. Houses differ.",
      "Before they leave",
      "If you are home, look at the pan, the relief pipe, and the unions. Ask where the new shutoff is. Keep the manual and any permit paper. If a leak shows the next day, call and say the install is new.",
      "Installation is the service named on the company website. A leaking tank and a planned upgrade use the same phone number.",
      ["Photo the heater label.","Measure a tight doorway.","Ask how long hot water will be off.","Keep the manual."])
    A("relief-pipe","The pipe on a relief valve","Water heaters","water-heaters",
      "Why a relief valve needs an open discharge pipe.",
      "A safety outlet",
      "The valve opens if temperature or pressure climbs too high. The pipe on it should run downhill, stay open, and end where a blast of hot water will not hit someone. Do not cap it. Do not add a valve to it.",
      "A drip may mean the valve is doing its job because pressure is high, or it may mean the valve is worn. Replacing the valve without asking why it opened can leave the pressure problem in place.",
      "If it is running hard",
      "If you can do it safely, shut the heater's energy off and shut the cold inlet to the heater if you know that valve. A fully open relief valve moves a lot of hot water. Then call.",
      "Describe a drip versus a stream. Water heater installation is the listed service that covers this equipment.",
      ["Never cap a relief pipe.","Note drip versus stream.","Shut heater energy if the flow is heavy and you can do it safely.","Call on a weekday."])
    A("rusty-hot-water","Rusty hot water","Water heaters","water-heaters",
      "How to tell heater rust from pipe rust when hot water runs brown.",
      "A bucket test",
      "Run the cold until it is clear, then run the hot into a white bucket. If only the hot is brown, the heater or the hot piping is the source. If the cold is brown too, the shared supply is involved.",
      "Water that clears in a minute and stays clear is different from brown water every morning. Do not drink water that is rusty. Use clear cold water for drinking until you know the cause.",
      "Repair or replacement",
      "A tank leaking through a rusty shell is a replacement. A tank that is discolored inside and still dry on the outside may be near the end. The visit should say whether you are buying an inspection, a repair, or an installation.",
      "Read the age off the label if you can. Gas and electric labels both help.",
      ["Compare hot and cold in a white bucket.","Note whether the color clears.","Do not drink rusty water.","Read the heater label."])
    A("toilet-supply","The supply line at a toilet","Fixtures","toilet-repair",
      "The small line to the toilet tank, a common urgent leak.",
      "Always under pressure",
      "The line from the wall valve to the tank is at full house pressure. When it fails, water sprays until that valve or the main is closed. Turn the angle stop clockwise. If it will not stop, use the main. Guest baths get less attention, so the line ages unseen.",
      "Replace lines that are bulged or crusted before they fail. If the stop still works, it is a small toilet repair. If the stop fails too, the visit is a little larger.",
      "Remodels",
      "New work on an old stop is when weak stops fail. If a bathroom was just rebuilt and the stop is original, say so. Ask for new stops in the scope while the room is open.",
      "Shut the spray off, then call (541) 614-1344 if you cannot isolate it.",
      ["Close the angle stop.","Use the main if the stop fails.","Replace bulged lines.","Add new stops to remodel scopes."])
    A("sink-trap","A leak under the sink trap","Drains","drain-services",
      "Loose slip nuts, cracked plastic, and thin metal traps.",
      "The common drip",
      "Plastic slip nuts loosen. A quarter turn by hand, with the sink empty, sometimes stops a weep. A cracked washer, a tailpiece bottomed out in the trap, or a cross-threaded nut will not seal. Forcing plastic splits it.",
      "Water only when the dishwasher runs may be the hose, not the trap. Water only when the sprayer is used is the sprayer. Have someone run each thing while you watch.",
      "Old metal",
      "Chrome traps can look fine while the metal is paper thin. If it flexes, it is due. Put a bucket under it before you loosen anything. If a chemical opener was used in the last day, wear gloves or leave the trap for the plumber.",
      "A photo under the sink shows which parts to bring. Drain and sink service includes this.",
      ["Tighten plastic nuts by hand.","Test the dishwasher and sprayer separately.","Bucket a rusty metal trap.","Warn about chemical opener."])
    A("disposals","Disposals and the kitchen drain","Drains","kitchen-plumbing",
      "What a disposal can do, and when the line past it is the problem.",
      "Not a trash can",
      "A disposal handles small soft scraps. Bones, large amounts of peel, grease, and pasta are how kitchens get calls. If it jams, many units have a hex socket underneath. Use it with the power off. If the key will not turn, stop. A humming motor left on will overheat.",
      "If both bowls are slow and the unit spins freely, the clog is downstream. A leak from the bottom housing means the unit is done. A leak at the sink flange is a seal. Those are different repairs.",
      "New units",
      "The dishwasher hose lands on a side inlet. A knock-out plug left in place blocks that hose and floods the machine. If the flood started right after a new disposal, say so.",
      "Kitchen plumbing is listed. Say whether the unit leaks, jams, or the sink is slow.",
      ["Cut power before using a hex key.","Keep bones and grease out.","Separate a jam from a slow line.","Mention a new disposal if the dishwasher floods."])
    A("washer-valves","Washer valves that will not shut","Fixtures","faucet-repair",
      "Why laundry valves fail on the day a hose fails.",
      "They sit still for years",
      "Washer valves are rarely turned, so mineral deposits freeze the stems. A handle that spins without stopping water is a failed stop. If a hose bursts, you may not have a working local valve. The main shutoff is the backup. Find it before you need it.",
      "Replacing the valves is easier when the machine can slide out. Some closets have almost no room. Say so when you book.",
      "Hoses and long trips",
      "Replace a bulged hose. Braid is not eternal. If the washer valves are original and you leave for weeks, consider shutting the house main as part of closing.",
      "Describe a spray versus a drip. Weekday hours end at 3:30 p.m.",
      ["Find the main before a hose fails.","Replace bulged hoses.","Note if the machine cannot slide.","Say if the stops spin freely."])
    A("wells","Wells and the house plumbing","Pipes","pipe-inspection",
      "How to separate a well problem from indoor plumbing when pressure drops.",
      "What you hear",
      "Inland houses around Siletz, Rose Lodge, Beaver, and Hebo are often on wells. A pressure tank and a switch sit in a well house or garage. If the pump never starts, that is often a well contractor or an electrician. If the pump runs and a house pipe leaks, that is plumbing. Say which behavior you hear.",
      "A clogged sediment filter can drop pressure in the house. Note the cartridge if you already maintain it. If the housing is cracked, stop.",
      "The house side",
      "Once usable pressure is at the tank, indoor fixtures and leaks are ordinary plumbing. The company does not list well drilling. Do not expect a pump pull unless a written estimate says so.",
      "Give gate codes and a plan for dogs. Rural drives need clear directions.",
      ["Say whether the pump is running.","Mention filters.","Separate well equipment from indoor leaks.","Share gate and dog details."])
    A("slab-or-crawl","Slab floors and crawlspaces","Pipes","pipe-inspection",
      "Why the foundation type changes a leak search.",
      "Under a crawlspace",
      "Many older beach houses are on a crawlspace or on piers. Lines are visible if you can get under them, unless insulation hides them. Clearance decides the labor. Say if it is a belly crawl.",
      "Some newer homes and additions are on a slab. A hot line leaking under a slab can warm the floor in a stripe. The repair may be a reroute overhead rather than a trench through concrete. That choice belongs in the estimate.",
      "Additions",
      "A house can have both: a crawlspace original and a slab addition. Bathrooms added on porches sometimes have long, flat drains. Mention the addition.",
      "Photos of the hatch and of any warm floor stripe help. Pipe inspection is the listed way to look before opening the wrong bay.",
      ["Say crawlspace, slab, or both.","Describe clearance.","Note an added bathroom.","Photo a warm stripe on a slab."])
    A("gurgling","Gurgling drains and vents","Drains","drain-services",
      "What a gurgle usually means about air in the drain system.",
      "Air has to follow the water",
      "Drains need air behind the water or they pull it through the nearest trap. A toilet that gurgles when a sink empties often means a blocked vent or a vent that was never there. Wind can pack a roof terminal. Ice can close one inland on a hard freeze.",
      "Look at a vent from the ground. Do not walk a steep wet roof to clear it.",
      "Other causes",
      "A partial main clog gurgles too. If fixtures are also slow, say both things. An island sink added without a proper vent will gurgle from the first week. Mention recent work.",
      "Drain service is the listed category. The visit should include the vent if the freeze or the remodel is part of the story.",
      ["Note which fixture gurgles when another runs.","Stay off a wet roof.","Say if drains are also slow.","Mention a recent remodel."])
    A("garage-heater","Water heaters in garages","Water heaters","water-heaters",
      "Damp floors, vehicles, and air around a garage water heater.",
      "Give it room",
      "Garages are damp, they get bumped by cars, and they run colder than the house. A pan with a drain is worth having. Gas heaters need combustion air and an intact vent. Do not store fuel against the heater, and do not box it in with shelves that block air.",
      "If the heater sits on a stand, say so. Ask the plumber what the local rule expects for height. Do not take a height from a blog post as if it were your permit.",
      "Call items",
      "Rust at the base, a scorched panel, a kinked gas connector, or a vent that has separated are weekday call items. If you smell gas, leave and call the gas utility.",
      "A photo from across the garage shows clearances. Installation is a listed service.",
      ["Keep storage off the heater.","Note a stand, a pan, and the fuel.","Do not block combustion air.","Use the gas utility for a gas odor."])
    A("two-bath-cottage","A cottage with two bathrooms","Remodels","bathroom-plumbing",
      "What changes when a small coast house gains a second bath.",
      "Shared pipes",
      "The second bath often shares a drain and a vent with the first. Slow drains and gurgles show up when both are used. Shower valves added later may sit in walls that now take the wind.",
      "A heater sized for one bath runs out when two showers start. That is not always a failed heater. Say how many people are in the house and whether hot water fails only at peak times.",
      "Rebuilding one of them",
      "If you are rebuilding the second bath, decide whether the toilet stays on the same wall. Ask how the sequence keeps one bathroom usable. Bathroom remodel is the listed service.",
      "Cottages in Lincoln City, Rockaway Beach, and Manzanita often have an original bath plus an addition. Say which room was added.",
      ["Say how many baths run at once.","Describe peak hot-water loss.","Keep one bath usable if you can.","Point out which bath was added."])
    A("filters","Filters, regulators, and weak pressure","Pipes","pipe-inspection",
      "Restrictions that mimic a failed plumbing system.",
      "Look at the simple parts first",
      "A sediment filter that has never been changed can close a house down. A failing pressure regulator can drop pressure over months. A main valve bumped half closed during storage work makes every fixture weak.",
      "If you change a cartridge, shut the water and have a towel ready. If the housing is cloudy with age or the valve above it weeps, stop. A cracked housing becomes a supply leak.",
      "Where the weakness starts",
      "Strong flow at an outdoor bib and weak flow indoors means the restriction is inside. Weak flow at the bib too means look upstream: the meter valve, the regulator, the well, or a leak in the yard. A meter that spins with every faucet off means water is leaving somewhere.",
      "Say what you already checked. Pipe inspection fits once the simple restrictions are ruled out.",
      ["Look at filter cartridges.","Compare an outdoor bib with an indoor faucet.","Watch the meter with fixtures off.","Stop if a filter housing is cracked."])
    A("ceiling-stain","A ceiling stain under a bath","Hiring","residential-plumbing",
      "How to limit damage from a stain under a tub, shower, or toilet.",
      "Active or old",
      "A dry ring may be old. A growing stain, a sag, or a drip is active. Stop using the fixture above. Shut the toilet angle stop if the toilet is overhead. Leave a shower off. Put a bucket under the drip. A bulging ceiling is holding water. Standing to the side if you relieve it is messy. If you are unsure, wait.",
      "The fixture above might be a supply, a valve, a wax ring, or a shower pan. Say which fixture sits over the stain. Those are different repairs.",
      "People and power",
      "Keep people out of the room below. On a rental, call the owner and the plumber. If water is near a light, leave that breaker off only if you can reach the panel from a dry floor. The weekday phone is (541) 614-1344.",
      "Photos from above and below belong in the email.",
      ["Stop using the fixture above.","Bucket the drip.","Keep people out of the room below.","Kill power only from a dry floor."])
    A("spinning-meter","A meter that spins with fixtures off","Pipes","pipe-inspection",
      "Using the water meter to tell whether a supply leak is running.",
      "The test",
      "Turn every fixture off, including the ice maker if you can, and watch the meter. A small dial that still turns means water is moving. Toilets are the usual cause. Dye the tanks. If the toilets are quiet and the meter still moves, look for a leak under the house or in the yard.",
      "Do not remove the meter. It belongs to the utility. Reading it is fine. Then shut the house valve and look again. If the meter stops, the leak is on the house side of that valve. If it keeps spinning, the leak is between the meter and the house valve.",
      "Who fixes the street",
      "That test is worth more than guessing. Call with the result. The company lists residential plumbing and pipe inspection. It does not list repairs to the utility main in the street.",
      "A dry yard with a wet crawlspace usually means the house side. A wet patch in the yard is a different clue.",
      ["Watch the meter with fixtures off.","Dye-test the toilets.","Repeat the test with the house valve closed.","Do not remove the meter."])
    A("fridge-line","Refrigerator water lines","Fixtures","kitchen-plumbing",
      "The small line behind a fridge, a quiet kitchen flood.",
      "Old valves and brittle tubing",
      "Piercing valves and stiff plastic tubing leak behind the fridge until the floor cups. A real angle stop and a braided line, secured so it does not kink when the fridge rolls out, is the durable setup. Watch the line when you pull the fridge forward.",
      "Stops hide in the next cabinet, in a crawlspace, or behind the unit. Find yours on a calm day. If a line is spraying, shut that valve or the house main.",
      "In a new kitchen",
      "Put the fridge stop on the list with the sink faucet. A pot filler and a fridge line are both easy to forget until the cabinets arrive. Kitchen plumbing scopes should name them.",
      "Email a photo of the valve you have. If there is no valve, say that.",
      ["Find the fridge shutoff now.","Avoid pinching the line.","Replace piercing valves.","Include the fridge line in a remodel scope."])
    A("tub-to-shower","Changing a tub to a shower","Remodels","bathroom-plumbing",
      "Drain, valve, and pan questions when a bathtub becomes a shower.",
      "Not a same-day fixture swap by default",
      "A tub drain and a shower drain are not always at the same height. A two-handle tub valve with no pressure balance is a poor shower valve. The estimate should say whether the valve, the trap, and the drain are all included. Bathroom remodel work is listed.",
      "The plumber sets the drain and the valve. The pan or liner has to meet that drain. Ask who waterproofs, and ask for a test before the wall closes. Tile over a flat pan will not drain.",
      "One bathroom",
      "If this is the only bath, ask how the household gets through the work. If the floor is already soft, the scope is bigger than a fixture swap. Say so at the start. If you already bought the valve, share the model.",
      "Have the parts chosen before the old tub comes out.",
      ["Ask if the valve will be replaced.","Clarify who waterproofs the pan.","Plan around a single bathroom.","Share the fixture model."])
    A("scald","Scald complaints in baths","Fixtures","faucet-repair",
      "Heater setting and shower valves, especially with children or guests.",
      "Two devices",
      "The heater sets the hottest water in the pipes. The shower valve mixes it with cold. A very high heater setting can still scald through a working valve if someone turns it full hot. A failed balance cartridge can scald when a toilet flush drops the cold side. Both deserve a look if someone was nearly burned.",
      "Many valves have a limit stop inside the handle. It is a backup, not a reason to ignore a broken cartridge. Faucet repair includes that work.",
      "Rentals",
      "Guests do not know which handle surges. Fix a valve that goes hot before the next check-in, and keep the estimate with the property notes. Weekday hours are the window.",
      "If someone is burned, get medical care. Then call about the valve.",
      ["Take a scald report seriously.","Ask about the handle limit stop.","Fix surge-hot valves before guests return.","Get medical care for a real burn."])
    A("groundwater","Standing water that is not a pipe leak","Coastal homes","pipe-inspection",
      "How to tell groundwater from plumbing, and which of those this company lists.",
      "Plumbing water can be switched",
      "Supply leaks stop when the right valve closes. Drain leaks follow fixture use. Groundwater does not. After days of coast rain, a crawlspace can take water through vents and low soil while every pipe is dry. Pipe inspection helps when you cannot tell.",
      "Sump pumps and foundation drains are not named on the BBB profile or the company website. This site will not pretend they are. If water continues with the main shut and no fixture in use, talk to a drainage contractor about site water.",
      "Both at once",
      "Valley houses and river houses sometimes have wet ground and a corroded pipe in that damp air. Describe both. Photos of the water, the nearest pipe, and the downspouts help the person you call.",
      "A fixture leak is still a plumbing call even if the crawlspace is also wet from rain.",
      ["Test by shutting the main.","Note recent heavy rain.","Keep drainage and plumbing as separate questions.","Photo downspouts and the wet bay."])
    A("owner-supplied","Fixtures you already bought","Fixtures","faucet-repair",
      "What to have on site when you supply the faucet, toilet, or valve.",
      "The whole box",
      "Bring every piece, including hardware and instructions. A faucet missing a washer will not seal. A valve missing a cartridge stops the job. Keep the box until the work is done. The plumber did not choose the quality of an owner-supplied fixture.",
      "Some faucets need a deck plate to cover extra holes. Count the holes in the sink. A three-hole sink and a single-hole faucet need that plate.",
      "Warranties",
      "The maker warrants the fixture. Labor terms are whatever the estimate states. This site does not create a warranty. Ask when you book.",
      "Email the model number to office@plumbing101.net ahead of the day. Faucet, bath, and kitchen installation are listed services.",
      ["Keep the full box.","Count the sink holes.","Email the model number.","Ask how labor is described on the estimate."])
    A("pipe-noise","Banging and whistling pipes","Pipes","pipe-inspection",
      "Water hammer and whistling, in plain language.",
      "A bang at the end of a cycle",
      "A bang when a washer, dishwasher, or ice maker valve closes is water hammer. The moving water stops all at once. Loose pipe can slap a joist. An arrestor near the quick-closing valve is the usual fix. Old air chambers waterlog and stop helping.",
      "Whistling is often a partly closed valve, a clogged aerator, or a toilet fill valve. Clean an aerator before you assume the piping is bad.",
      "A new drip",
      "A bang with a new drip means a joint moved. Shut the water if the drip is active. Pipe inspection is the listed way to look at supports and tired valves.",
      "Name the appliance that triggers the noise.",
      ["Note which appliance causes the bang.","Clean aerators first.","Look for a new drip after a bang.","Ask about arrestors."])
    A("dishwasher-flood","A dishwasher that floods the kitchen","Drains","kitchen-plumbing",
      "When a dishwasher flood is really a sink drain, and when it is the machine.",
      "Watch the sink too",
      "If the sink is slow, start there. Dishwashers empty into that system. A clogged tailpiece sends water out an air gap or around the door. If the sink drains freely and the machine still leaks at the door, the seal or the pump may be an appliance issue. Say what you see so the right trade comes.",
      "Under the sink, the dishwasher hose should rise to the underside of the counter before it drops. A new sink that skipped that loop misbehaves immediately.",
      "Stop the repeats",
      "Do not run the machine again to test it. Water swells a cabinet floor fast. Unplug the unit or shut its breaker if you can do that without standing in water.",
      "Photos of the hose path help a kitchen plumbing call.",
      ["Stop rerunning the cycle.","Check whether the sink is slow.","Look for a high loop.","Keep the cabinet floor dry."])
    A("rental-turnover","Plumbing between guest stays","Coastal homes","residential-plumbing",
      "A short turnover list for coastal rentals, tied to listed services.",
      "Ten minutes",
      "Flush each toilet and listen for a valve that keeps running. Run each sink and look underneath. Run the shower and confirm it shuts off. Check the heater pan. Look at washer hoses if guests can reach them.",
      "Sand in outdoor showers and hair in tubs are the drain items. The company is closed Saturday and Sunday. The hours are Monday through Friday, 8:30 a.m. to 3:30 p.m. Book known repairs inside that week.",
      "A house note",
      "A page about wipes, grease, and the main shutoff helps guests. It does not replace a repair you already know you need. If a toilet rocks or a faucet drips, schedule it.",
      "Keep CCB 232697 on the property work order. Toilets, faucets, drains, and heaters are the relevant listed services.",
      ["Listen after each flush.","Look under sinks every turnover.","Clear sand and hair.","Schedule repairs on weekdays."])
    A("old-metals","Old faucets and drinking water","Fixtures","faucet-repair",
      "A careful note on old plumbing metals, without pretending to be a lab.",
      "Do not guess",
      "Some older solders and lines are not what you would install now. You cannot identify them by glancing at a joint. If a house is old and water sits overnight, using fresh cold water for drinking is a conservative habit. It is not a test. A certified lab test is a test. This company is not a water laboratory.",
      "Replacing a kitchen faucet and its supply lines removes some old material at the point of use. It is not a whole-house re-pipe unless that is the scope.",
      "Filters",
      "Read the certification on a filter box for what it reduces. A carbon filter is not a substitute for pipe that is failing mechanically.",
      "Say whether you want faucet supplies or a whole-house re-pipe. They are different estimates.",
      ["Use a lab if you need a test.","Run fresh cold water after long stagnation.","Read filter certifications.","Name the scope."])
    A("stuck-valve","A valve that will not turn","Pipes","residential-plumbing",
      "What to do when a shutoff is stuck, without snapping the stem.",
      "Replace it. Do not cheat it.",
      "Old gate valves seize. A cheater bar snaps the stem, and then you have no valve and maybe a new leak. If the drip is small, leave the stuck valve and book a replacement on a weekday. If water is spraying, go to the next valve upstream, usually the house main or the meter.",
      "Angle stops fail the same way. A faucet replacement is a good time to change them if the pipe is sound. Put that on the estimate.",
      "Handle clearance",
      "A ball valve needs room to turn a quarter turn. A handle that hits the cabinet gets left half closed, which whistles and cuts flow. Mention tight cabinets.",
      "Describe the leak that made you touch the valve, not only the stuck handle.",
      ["Do not use a cheater bar.","Go upstream if water is spraying.","Ask for new stops with a new faucet.","Leave swing room for a ball valve."])
    A("sulfur-smell","Sulfur smell on hot water only","Water heaters","water-heaters",
      "Why a rotten-egg smell only in hot water points at the heater.",
      "Hot versus cold",
      "If the cold smells normal and the hot smells like sulfur, the reaction is in the heater. Bacteria can interact with an anode in some waters and produce that odor in the tank. It is different from a sewer smell at a floor drain that does not care whether the water is hot.",
      "A plumber may change the anode type or flush the tank, depending on the water and the tank. Do not pour cleaners into a tank. Do not leave the anode opening uncapped.",
      "When it is a drain",
      "A smell at one shower that leaves after you add water is a dry trap. A smell with slow drains is a drain or vent issue. Do the hot-versus-cold test before you buy parts.",
      "Water heater service is listed. Describe the test you ran.",
      ["Compare hot and cold.","Do not pour chemicals into the tank.","Rule out a dry trap.","Report the test."])
    A("buying-a-house","Plumbing questions for buyers","Inspections","pipe-inspection",
      "What a buyer can ask a plumber to look at on a weekday schedule.",
      "A practical list",
      "Heater age and condition, active leaks, exposed galvanized pipe, toilet bases, hose bibs, and the main shutoff are a fair list. A drain camera is a separate scope if the disclosure mentions backups. Pipe inspection and evaluation is the service name on the BBB profile.",
      "Deadlines are often tighter than posted hours. This company is open until 3:30 p.m. on weekdays. A weekend open house does not create weekend plumbing hours.",
      "You are hiring someone",
      "Check CCB 232697 on the state site, including the complaint line and the current status. This is not a full home inspection. A plumber looks at plumbing.",
      "Ask for the findings in writing.",
      ["List the heater, leaks, exposed pipe, and the shutoff.","Book inside weekday hours.","Add a camera only if backups are disclosed.","Read the CCB record."])
    A("shop-heater","Water heaters in a small business","Water heaters","commercial-plumbing",
      "What to tell a plumber about a shop or cafe water heater.",
      "Use sets the size",
      "A shop with one restroom is not a cafe running dishes all day. Commercial plumbing and water heater installation are both listed. Fuel, venting, and the fixture list decide the equipment. Mention a dishwasher if there is one.",
      "Closets in older highway buildings are often too small or too blocked. Photos of the closet, the vent, and the doorway prevent a wasted trip.",
      "Daytime access",
      "Company hours end at 3:30 p.m. If the manager is only on site in the evening, arrange a daytime unlock. Say who will be there.",
      "No price is printed here. The estimate follows the room and the fixtures.",
      ["Describe restrooms and dishwashers.","Photo the closet and the vent.","Arrange daytime access.","Separate a shop sink from a full kitchen."])
    A("iced-vent","Vents in a cold snap","Drains","drain-services",
      "Slow drains during a hard freeze can be an iced vent terminal.",
      "Where it shows up",
      "Gurgling and toilets that burp during a freeze can be ice on a vent, especially in Hebo, Eddyville, and other colder spots. Beach towns see it less, though a packed vent can act the same. If the symptom vanishes when the weather warms, that is a clue.",
      "Do not carry boiling water onto a roof. The fall is worse than the clog. A plumber with real access can clear a terminal, or find that the blockage was never ice.",
      "Inside",
      "Use fixtures gently. If one overflows, stop. If sewage stands in a floor drain, stop water use and call. Mention the freeze so the visit includes the vent.",
      "Drain service is the listed category. Say the town and how cold the night was.",
      ["Tie the symptom to the freeze.","Stay off the roof.","Stop using a fixture that overflows.","Name the town."])
    A("spare-bath","The bathroom nobody uses","Fixtures","toilet-repair",
      "Supply lines and flappers in the spare bath of a second home.",
      "Out of sight",
      "A spare bath can sit for months. The flapper sticks, the supply line hardens, and the trap dries. The first guest finds the running toilet. Include this room when you open the house for the season.",
      "If it has a shutoff, turn it once a year. A valve that will not move should be replaced on a planned visit, not during a spill.",
      "Small repairs",
      "A dripping tub faucet in that room wastes hot water and stains the tub. It is still a faucet repair if the valve will come apart. Toilet repair is listed as well.",
      "Put the spare bath on the same list as the kitchen.",
      ["Test the spare-bath stop once a year.","Refill the trap with water.","Listen for a running flapper.","Include the room when you open the house."])
    A("estimate-email","What to put in an estimate email","Hiring","residential-plumbing",
      "A short format for writing office@plumbing101.net.",
      "Lead with the facts",
      "Name, callback number, town, and whether it is a house, a rental, or a small shop. Then one sentence: supply leak under the kitchen sink, no hot water, slow main-floor drains, or a heater sitting in a pan of water. Say if the water is already shut off.",
      "Three photos are enough: the scene, the fitting or label, and any damage. The contact form on this site opens an email to the same address.",
      "Timing",
      "Messages are handled inside Monday through Friday, 8:30 a.m. to 3:30 p.m. A note sent Friday evening waits unless you reached a person by phone. The phone is (541) 614-1344.",
      "Do not send bank numbers or copies of identification.",
      ["Lead with the town and the symptom.","Say if the water is off.","Send a few clear photos.","Use the phone for an active spray."])
    A("repeated-pinholes","When pinholes keep coming back","Pipes","repiping",
      "How a string of pinhole leaks changes a repair into a re-pipe conversation.",
      "One hole is a repair",
      "A single pinhole in sound, accessible copper can be cut out and coupled. The third pinhole in a year, or a run that is thin along its length, means couplings are a delay. Re-piping is the listed service for replacing the system. The client note on the company site about a beach-home re-pipe is that scale of work.",
      "Water chemistry, velocity, and other causes are things a plumber looks at in person. A page cannot name yours. Pipe inspection is how someone sees the remaining pipe.",
      "Keep a log",
      "Dates, rooms, and photos make the pattern clear. This site is not an insurance guide. Call while the current leak is stable and the main is holding, so the visit can land on a weekday.",
      "Ask for a look at the pipe that has not failed yet.",
      ["Log each pinhole.","Do not plan on endless couplings.","Ask for an inspection of the rest.","Schedule while the main is holding."])
    A("garage-floor-drain","Garage floor drains","Drains","drain-services",
      "Dry traps, smells, and what must stay out of a garage floor drain.",
      "Refill the trap",
      "A floor drain nobody uses loses its water seal. The smell is sewer gas, and the first fix is water. If the smell returns every week, a primer may have failed or a vent may be pulling the trap dry. That diagnosis is drain service.",
      "Do not pour paint, oil, solvent, or concrete slurry into the drain. Sand from beach gear belongs in a trap you can lift, not in a buried line.",
      "Backups",
      "A floor drain that rises when the washer runs is tied to that line or to the main. Stop the washer. If sewage stands in the garage, keep people and pets out and call. Describe it clearly so weekday scheduling reflects it.",
      "Hours are Monday through Friday, 8:30 a.m. to 3:30 p.m.",
      ["Refill a smelling drain with water.","Keep paint and oil out.","Stop the washer if the drain rises.","Keep pets out of sewage."])
    A("angle-stop-drip","A drip at an angle stop","Fixtures","faucet-repair",
      "The small valve under a sink or toilet that weeps when it is finally turned.",
      "Years of sitting still",
      "The washer inside hardens. The first time the valve is closed for a faucet repair, the stem weeps. Sometimes it weeps on its own. A slow drip can wait in a bowl for a weekday visit. A stream means use the house main.",
      "You do not need the part name on the phone. A photo is enough. Faucet work is a natural time to replace stops, because the water is already part of the conversation.",
      "The pipe in the wall",
      "If the pipe leaving the wall is crusted steel, say so. The repair may be a new stop or a short piece of pipe. Forcing threads on rotten pipe makes a bigger leak.",
      "Email the photo to office@plumbing101.net with the town.",
      ["Bowl a slow drip.","Use the main for a stream.","Photo the pipe at the wall.","Plan new stops with faucet work."])
    A("only-bathroom","Remodeling the only bathroom","Remodels","bathroom-plumbing",
      "Planning the days a one-bath cottage is without its bathroom.",
      "Sequence",
      "The household still needs water and a toilet. A plumber can sometimes keep a toilet in service while a shower is rebuilt, and sometimes cannot. Ask before demo. Bathroom remodel is listed work, and the sequence belongs on the estimate.",
      "Hours end at 3:30 p.m. A demo that starts late can leave the water off overnight. Decide that on purpose.",
      "Parts on site",
      "Have the valve, the toilet, and the shower parts there before the old ones come out, or confirm the plumber is bringing them. A missing valve extends the outage. Keep a path clear so the old fixture can leave.",
      "Tell anyone sharing a wall that the room will be open.",
      ["Ask if a toilet can stay in service.","Match demo to weekday hours.","Have the new fixtures on site.","Keep a carry path clear."])
    A("label-valves","Label the valves","Hiring","residential-plumbing",
      "A small habit that helps guests, owners, and plumbers in a second home.",
      "What to tag",
      "Tag the main shutoff, the heater cold inlet, the washer valves, and any indoor shutoff for an outdoor shower. Write the off direction. In a rental, put the same notes in the house book. Family members trading weeks in Pacific City, Neskowin, or Lincoln City do not all know the crawlspace.",
      "A gate valve feels endless if nobody explains it, and a guest may leave it half closed.",
      "Correct the map",
      "A label that calls a hose-bib feed the main will send the next person to the wrong valve. Fix the tag when you learn the difference. A labeled house is faster to make safe. It does not replace a repair.",
      "A photo of the valves is worth keeping in your own email.",
      ["Tag the main, the heater inlet, and the washer valves.","Write the off direction.","Put the same notes in a rental book.","Fix a wrong label."])
    A("after-the-repair","After the plumber leaves","Hiring","residential-plumbing",
      "What to look at in the first days after a repair or a new heater.",
      "One more look",
      "Before you pile storage on the hatch, look at the repair with a flashlight. A fitting should be dry. A new heater should be full, with an open relief pipe that is not streaming. Toilets should finish filling and stop. If something is wet, call while the visit is fresh.",
      "Debris in aerators is normal for a short time after pipe work. Clean the screens once. If they clog all week, call.",
      "Paper",
      "Keep the estimate, any permit, and the heater manual together. The license number on the contract should read 232697. The state site is where you recheck the record later.",
      "The next morning, the phone and email are the same: (541) 614-1344 and office@plumbing101.net.",
      ["Look at the fitting before you restock.","Clean aerators once after pipe work.","File the estimate and the manual.","Call if a new fitting weeps."])
    A("mineral-spots","Spots on fixtures","Fixtures","faucet-repair",
      "How mineral film differs from a faucet that is actually leaking.",
      "Wipe, then watch",
      "Mineral film on a dry faucet wipes off with a cleaner the maker allows. It does not need a plumber. A drip that returns while you watch is a leak. Faucet repair is for the drip.",
      "White crust on a hose bib or a heater fitting can be a slow weep that dries as it forms. If the crust is damp underneath, it is a leak. If it is dry and stable, clean it and watch for a week.",
      "Wells",
      "Some inland wells leave more mineral than a city supply. A filter question and a leak question can both be real. Crust that keeps growing on many fittings is a reason to talk about pipe inspection. Do not sand the finish off a faucet to chase spots.",
      "Call when water returns, not when a dry spot remains.",
      ["Wipe and watch.","Call if water returns.","See whether crust is damp.","Use the cleaner the fixture maker allows."])
    A("meter-to-house","The line from the meter to the house","Pipes","repiping",
      "What you can observe about the buried supply without digging a guess hole.",
      "A wet patch and a meter",
      "A soft wet patch between the meter and the house, with no rain to explain it, can be a service-line leak. Confirm with the meter: fixtures off, house valve closed, dial still moving. That pattern points at the buried section. Call 811 before anyone digs.",
      "Re-piping and residential plumbing are listed. A buried service line should be named on the estimate if that is the work. It is not a faucet repair. The company does not list utility mains in the street.",
      "Tight lots",
      "Coast lots are small. Water lines share ground with other utilities. Valley properties around Siletz and Rose Lodge can have long runs. Length changes the job. Do not pick a spot and excavate on a hunch.",
      "Describe the wet spot, the meter result, and the rough distance.",
      ["Do the meter test with the house valve closed.","Call 811 before any digging.","Do not excavate a guess.","Tell the plumber the rough length."])
